{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\n\noutput = Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/mind/runs/run-20260426T144402Z--et8g2bwn96/semantic/ingest_extraction.json')\nsource_id = 'source-04-mind-the-hype-a-critical-evaluation-and-prescriptive-agenda-for-research-on-mind-w2761197918--79c0d54db997'\n\nclaims = [\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-mindfulness-hype-exceeds-evidence--96cb023af909',\n        'text': 'Mindfulness and meditation have become widely promoted in clinical, educational, corporate, military, and media contexts despite an evidence base that remains uneven and vulnerable to exaggeration.',\n        'short_title': 'Hype exceeds evidence',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Fig. 1 tracks scientific and news media articles on mindfulness and/or meditation by year from 1970 to 2015, using Scopus for empirical scientific articles and LexisNexis for newspaper media pieces.',\n            'During the past two decades, mindfulness meditation has gone from being a fringe topic of scientific investigation to being an occasional replacement for psychotherapy, tool of corporate well-being, widely implemented educational practice, and “key to building more resilient soldiers.”'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-mindfulness-definition-is-ambiguous--4f220365d6f4',\n        'text': 'The term mindfulness lacks a universally accepted technical definition and often conflates heterogeneous mental faculties, practices, interventions, and measurement targets.',\n        'short_title': 'Mindfulness is ambiguous',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'There is neither one universally accepted technical definition of “mindfulness” nor any broad agreement about detailed aspects of the underlying concept to which it refers.',\n            'A 5-minute meditation exercise from a popular phone application might be treated the same as a 3-month meditation retreat, and a self-report questionnaire might be equated with the characteristics of someone who has spent decades practicing.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-self-report-measures-lack-validity--56550542660a',\n        'text': 'Self-report mindfulness questionnaires often have weak construct validity because scale meanings, factor structures, item interpretation, social desirability, and introspective limitations vary across participants and practice histories.',\n        'short_title': 'Self reports lack validity',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Table 1 lists multiple mindfulness measures with different theoretical contexts and factor structures, including FMI, MAAS, KIMS, FFMQ, TMS, CAMS-R, PHLMS, SMQ, and SMS.',\n            'Several of these scales exhibit different factor structures and response properties between meditators and nonmeditators, as well as before and after mindfulness training.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-clinical-evidence-remains-preliminary--117717456958',\n        'text': 'Clinical evidence for mindfulness-based interventions remains preliminary because much intervention research has not advanced beyond early-stage efficacy designs and often lacks active controls or real-world practice tests.',\n        'short_title': 'Clinical evidence remains preliminary',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Dimidjian and Segal estimate that only 30% of research using mindfulness-based interventions has moved beyond Stage 1; 20% was Stage 2a, 9% Stage 2b, and only 1% outside research contexts.',\n            'MBIs compared to active controls were found to have a mixture of only moderate, low, or no efficacy, depending on the disorder being treated.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-therapeutic-benefits-are-condition-specific--201401200700',\n        'text': 'Mindfulness-based therapies may help some people and conditions, particularly some depression-related contexts, but they should not be treated as broadly superior to established treatments.',\n        'short_title': 'Benefits are condition specific',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'MBCT may be modestly helpful for some individuals with residual symptoms of depression, and some guidelines endorse MBCT for preventing relapse in remitted patients with three or more previous depressive episodes.',\n            'Head-to-head comparisons of MBIs to other evidence-based practices have resulted in mixed findings, and direct comparisons of MBIs to empirically established treatments are limited.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-adverse-effects-are-undermeasured--25fa20260426',\n        'text': 'Meditation-related adverse effects and opportunity costs are plausible, documented, and undermeasured, so safety claims based on passive reporting or absent evidence are unwarranted.',\n        'short_title': 'Adverse effects are undermeasured',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Meditation-related experiences serious or distressing enough to warrant additional treatment or medical attention have been reported in more than 20 published case reports or observational studies.',\n            'Compared to 100% of pharmacology trials, less than 25% of meditation trials actively assess adverse events, relying instead on spontaneous reporting.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-neuroimaging-requires-caution--201770958900',\n        'text': 'Neuroimaging evidence on meditation can suggest modest brain differences, but group averages, cross-sectional designs, cardiorespiratory artifacts, head motion, and interpretive oversimplification limit causal and practical conclusions.',\n        'short_title': 'Neuroimaging requires caution',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Representative pictures from fMRI and other neuroimaging methods do not clearly convey the complex chain of biological and computational steps that lead to inferences about brain structure and function.',\n            'Many neuroimaging studies have used cross-sectional designs, precluding possible inferences about underlying cause-and-effect relationships.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-research-needs-prescriptive-reform--79c0d54db997',\n        'text': 'Future mindfulness research should use precise terminology, stronger construct measures, active controls, preregistration, adverse-event monitoring, detailed intervention reporting, and cautious public communication.',\n        'short_title': 'Research needs reform',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Table 3 proposes study design features for mindfulness-based interventions, including teacher information, practice information, instructor adherence, control group use, randomization, adverse-event monitoring, participant criteria, prior meditation experience, and conflicts of interest.',\n            'Researchers should adopt more nuanced terminology, follow replication-crisis lessons such as preregistered experiments and open-science replications, and report contemplative-neuroscience findings with due modesty.'\n        ]\n    }\n]\n\nevidence_items = [\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-publication-and-media-growth--96cb023af909',\n        'title': 'Publication and Media Growth',\n        'excerpt': 'Fig. 1. Scientific and news media articles on mindfulness and/or meditation by year from 1970 to 2015. Empirical scientific articles with the term mindfulness or meditation in the abstract, title, or keywords were searched using Scopus; media pieces with the term mindfulness or meditation in newspapers were searched using LexisNexis with a similarity filter.',\n        'overview': 'This figure is the paper’s central descriptive evidence for the claim that mindfulness moved rapidly from a fringe research topic into a saturated scientific and media discourse. It does not by itself prove that public claims are false, but it establishes the scale and timing of attention that makes hype, overgeneralization, and communication failures scientifically important.',\n        'evidence_type': 'figure_result',\n        'claim_refs': ['0'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['Fig. 1', 'p. 37']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-mindfulness-measures-table--4f220365d6f4',\n        'title': 'Divergent Mindfulness Measures',\n        'excerpt': 'Table 1 lists mindfulness measures with different contexts and factors: FMI from Buddhist theory with one general factor; MAAS from self-determination theory with attentiveness and awareness; KIMS from dialectical behavior therapy with observing, describing, awareness, and acceptance; FFMQ with nonreactivity, observing, awareness, describing, and nonjudging; TMS with curiosity and decentering; CAMS-R with attention, present focus, awareness, and acceptance.',\n        'overview': 'The table functions as concrete evidence that mindfulness is not measured as a single settled construct. Multiple instruments use different theoretical backgrounds and factor structures, making it risky to treat questionnaire scores as interchangeable evidence for a common mental capacity or intervention mechanism.',\n        'evidence_type': 'table_result',\n        'claim_refs': ['1', '2'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['Table 1', 'p. 39']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-clinical-stage-distribution--117717456958',\n        'title': 'Early Clinical Stage Distribution',\n        'excerpt': 'Dimidjian and Segal estimate, using the NIH stage model for clinical science, that only 30% of research using mindfulness-based interventions has moved beyond Stage 1. The majority beyond Stage 1 was Stage 2a, compared to wait-list control or treatment as usual; 9% of the total was Stage 2b, compared to active control; only 1% was conducted outside research contexts.',\n        'overview': 'These proportions are concrete methodological evidence for the paper’s caution about clinical rollout. They show that the intervention literature was dominated by early refinement or weak comparison designs, with very little evidence from active-control or real-world implementation contexts.',\n        'evidence_type': 'statistical_result',\n        'claim_refs': ['3'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['p. 45']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-active-control-efficacy-profile--117717456959',\n        'title': 'Mixed Active-Control Efficacy',\n        'excerpt': 'In a review and meta-analysis commissioned by the U.S. Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality, mindfulness-based interventions compared to active controls had only moderate efficacy for anxiety, depression, and pain; low efficacy for stress and quality of life; and no effect or insufficient evidence for attention, positive mood, substance abuse, eating habits, sleep, and weight control.',\n        'overview': 'This evidence item qualifies popular therapeutic claims by separating domains with moderate evidence from domains with weak or absent evidence. It supports a condition-specific interpretation: mindfulness interventions may be useful in some contexts, but the source rejects broad claims of strong or universal clinical efficacy.',\n        'evidence_type': 'statistical_result',\n        'claim_refs': ['3', '4'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['p. 46']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-adverse-event-reporting-gap--25fa20260426',\n        'title': 'Adverse Event Reporting Gap',\n        'excerpt': 'Meditation-related experiences serious or distressing enough to warrant additional treatment or medical attention have been reported in more than 20 published case reports or observational studies, including psychosis, mania, depersonalization, anxiety, panic, traumatic-memory reexperiencing, and clinical deterioration. Less than 25% of meditation trials actively assess adverse events, compared to 100% of pharmacology trials.',\n        'overview': 'The case-report count and trial-monitoring comparison are the paper’s strongest safety evidence. They do not establish population incidence, but they directly undermine claims that meditation is risk-free and show why passive reporting is inadequate for estimating harms.',\n        'evidence_type': 'statistical_result',\n        'claim_refs': ['5'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['pp. 47-48']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-study-design-checklist--79c0d54db997',\n        'title': 'Mindfulness Study Design Checklist',\n        'excerpt': 'Table 3 lists study design features for a mindfulness-based intervention: teacher information, practice information, physical and social settings, overall duration, meeting frequency and length, types and percentages of formal practice, informal practice, logs, instructional materials, instructor adherence, control group use, randomization/allocation method, adverse events monitored, inclusion/exclusion criteria, prior meditation experience, and conflicts of interest.',\n        'overview': 'The checklist is a prescriptive artifact rather than an outcome result. It concretely specifies the reporting and design details needed to make mindfulness studies interpretable, comparable, and safer, especially when different interventions share the same umbrella label.',\n        'evidence_type': 'formal_argument',\n        'claim_refs': ['1', '3', '7'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['Table 3', 'p. 42']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-neuroimaging-confounds--201770958900',\n        'title': 'Neuroimaging Confound Limits',\n        'excerpt': 'The source identifies neuroimaging limits including group-average inference, pooling diverse practitioners, cross-sectional designs that preclude cause-and-effect conclusions, head movement, cardiorespiratory effects, vein-drain artifacts, and difficulty estimating practical significance of observed structural or functional brain changes.',\n        'overview': 'This evidence item grounds the paper’s warning that meditation brain images are not self-interpreting proof of clinical benefit or causal neural change. The source acknowledges modest meta-analytic findings while arguing that artifacts and design limits must be handled before practical conclusions are imported into treatment or public messaging.',\n        'evidence_type': 'formal_argument',\n        'claim_refs': ['6', '7'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['pp. 50-51']\n    }\n]\n\ndata = {\n    'source_date_inference': {\n        'confidence': 'high',\n        'date': '2018',\n        'origin': 'source_markdown_publication_date',\n        'rationale': 'The source markdown cover page and citation state Publication date: 2018 for the Perspectives on Psychological Science article.'\n    },\n    'source': {\n        'display_title': 'Mindfulness Research Needs Less Hype and More Rigor',\n        'authors': [\n            'Nicholas T. Van Dam',\n            'Marieke K. van Vugt',\n            'David R. Vago',\n            'Laura Schmalzl',\n            'Clifford D. Saron',\n            'Andrew Olendzki',\n            'Ted Meissner',\n            'Sara W. Lazar',\n            'Catherine E. Kerr',\n            'Jolie Gorchov',\n            'Kieran C. R. Fox',\n            'Brent A. Field',\n            'Willoughby B. Britton',\n            'Julie A. Brefczynski-Lewis',\n            'David E. Meyer'\n        ],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'canonical_title': 'Mind the Hype: A Critical Evaluation and Prescriptive Agenda for Research on Mindfulness and Meditation',\n        'doi': '10.1177/1745691617709589',\n        'publication': 'Perspectives on Psychological Science'\n    },\n    'claims': claims,\n    'evidence_items': evidence_items,\n    'relations': [\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'supports',\n            'src_claim_ref': '1',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '2',\n            'rationale': 'Ambiguous definitions make self-report measures harder to interpret as measures of a single construct.'\n        },\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'supports',\n            'src_claim_ref': '3',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '4',\n            'rationale': 'The preliminary clinical evidence base constrains claims about broad therapeutic benefit.'\n        },\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'supports',\n            'src_claim_ref': '5',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '0',\n            'rationale': 'Undermeasured adverse effects raise the stakes of public hype and overconfident implementation.'\n        },\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'derived_from',\n            'src_claim_ref': '7',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '1',\n            'rationale': 'The recommendation for precise terminology follows from the paper’s diagnosis of semantic ambiguity.'\n        },\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'derived_from',\n            'src_claim_ref': '7',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '3',\n            'rationale': 'The recommendation for stronger study design follows from the weaknesses in clinical intervention methodology.'\n        },\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'derived_from',\n            'src_claim_ref': '7',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '6',\n            'rationale': 'The recommendation for cautious public communication follows from the limits of neuroimaging interpretation.'\n        }\n    ],\n    'summary': 'Van Dam and colleagues argue that mindfulness research has been promoted faster than its evidence base can support. The source identifies semantic ambiguity, weak measurement, immature clinical trials, underreported adverse effects, and overinterpreted neuroimaging as major reasons for a more cautious and rigorous research agenda.',\n    'warnings': [\n        {\n            'code': 'task_context_date_unresolved',\n            'message': 'The task_context reported date=null, but the source markdown itself states a 2018 publication date; the output uses the markdown evidence.'\n        },\n        {\n            'code': 'markdown_layout_artifacts',\n            'message': 'The pdftotext markdown is usable but includes two-column layout interleaving in some passages; extracted claims rely on repeated, clear passages and table captions rather than ambiguous line joins.'\n        }\n    ],\n    'source_dossier': {\n        'summary_short': 'This article is a critical review and agenda-setting paper about mindfulness and meditation research. It argues that enthusiasm in media, clinical practice, education, and neuroscience has outrun the precision and strength of the evidence. The authors call for clearer terminology, stronger measures, better clinical designs, active monitoring of harms, and more cautious communication of brain-imaging findings.',\n        'summary_long': 'Mind the Hype evaluates the modern mindfulness movement from the standpoint of psychological science, clinical intervention research, adverse-event monitoring, and contemplative neuroscience. Its central argument is not that mindfulness is useless or harmful in general; rather, it argues that public and institutional claims about mindfulness often exceed what the evidence can responsibly support. The article first identifies a semantic problem: “mindfulness” can refer to mental traits, temporary states, formal meditation practices, intervention packages, self-report scales, or long-term contemplative training. Because those uses differ so much, findings about one form of practice or measurement cannot automatically generalize to another. The authors then show how this ambiguity affects measurement. Existing questionnaires come from different theoretical traditions, use different factor structures, and may be interpreted differently by meditators and nonmeditators. Clinical evidence receives a similarly cautious treatment. The review recognizes possible benefits for some depression-related and symptom domains, but it emphasizes that much intervention work remains early-stage, often uses wait-list or treatment-as-usual controls, and only rarely tests real-world implementation. Safety is another major theme: meditation-related adverse experiences have been documented, yet trials have often relied on passive reporting, making “no evidence of harm” a weak basis for public reassurance. Finally, the article warns that neuroimaging evidence is especially vulnerable to oversimplification because brain images depend on complex group-level, statistical, physiological, and design assumptions. The prescriptive agenda is therefore multipronged: define practices and constructs precisely, report intervention details, use active controls and preregistration, include skeptical and multidisciplinary teams, systematically assess adverse events, and communicate findings to the public without exaggerating benefits or certainty.',\n        'sections': [\n            {\n                'heading': 'Central Argument',\n                'body': 'The source argues that mindfulness research has generated genuine scientific interest, but that public enthusiasm, media messaging, and some applied rollouts have moved faster than the evidence. Its critical stance is balanced: mindfulness may help some people in some contexts, yet the authors reject claims that it is a broadly proven remedy or a risk-free replacement for established care.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-mindfulness-hype-exceeds-evidence--96cb023af909', 'claim-therapeutic-benefits-are-condition-specific--201401200700']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Definition Problem',\n                'body': 'A major source of confusion is the umbrella use of “mindfulness.” The paper treats semantic precision as a methodological requirement, because a brief phone-app exercise, an eight-week MBSR course, a self-report questionnaire, and decades of contemplative practice are not interchangeable evidence targets. Without differentiating practices, mechanisms, and measures, studies are difficult to compare.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-mindfulness-definition-is-ambiguous--4f220365d6f4']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Measurement Path',\n                'body': 'The discussion of questionnaires shows why simple mindfulness scores can mislead. The listed measures differ in origins and factor structures, and the authors highlight problems with introspection, social desirability, demand characteristics, and different item meanings before versus after training. The recommended path is to measure more specific cognitive, affective, behavioral, and biological capacities rather than treating mindfulness as a unitary latent trait.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-self-report-measures-lack-validity--56550542660a', 'claim-research-needs-prescriptive-reform--79c0d54db997']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Clinical Evidence',\n                'body': 'The article distinguishes limited, condition-specific promise from generalized clinical efficacy. It notes possible benefits in some depression-related contexts and some symptom outcomes, but the broader evidence base is constrained by early-stage designs, limited active controls, inconsistent intervention content, and very little research outside controlled research settings. The result is a call for caution before mainstream implementation.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-clinical-evidence-remains-preliminary--117717456958', 'claim-therapeutic-benefits-are-condition-specific--201401200700']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Safety and Harms',\n                'body': 'The source treats adverse effects as an underdeveloped research area rather than a settled objection to meditation. Case reports and observational studies document serious distressing experiences, while trial monitoring has often been weak. The authors also emphasize opportunity cost: overstated claims may divert vulnerable people from better established treatment or ordinary activities with known mental and physical benefits.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-adverse-effects-are-undermeasured--25fa20260426']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Neuroscience Limits',\n                'body': 'Contemplative neuroscience receives a nuanced assessment. Brain imaging can generate promising hypotheses and meta-analytic patterns, but the paper warns against translating colorful images into simple claims about benefit, causality, or clinical importance. Group averaging, cross-sectional comparisons, respiration, cardiac activity, head movement, and lifestyle differences can all affect interpretation.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-neuroimaging-requires-caution--201770958900']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Practical Interpretation',\n                'body': 'The practical lesson is reform rather than rejection. The authors want mindfulness research to adopt clearer taxonomies, stronger reporting checklists, active control groups, preregistration, multidisciplinary and skeptical collaboration, adverse-event assessment, and careful public communication. Readers should treat mindfulness as a family of practices requiring context-specific evidence, not as a single validated intervention with universal effects.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-research-needs-prescriptive-reform--79c0d54db997', 'claim-mindfulness-hype-exceeds-evidence--96cb023af909']\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n}\n\noutput.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\noutput.write_text(json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2) + '\\\\n', encoding='utf-8')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\n\noutput = Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/mind/runs/run-20260426T144402Z--et8g2bwn96/semantic/ingest_extraction.json')\nsource_id = 'source-04-mind-the-hype-a-critical-evaluation-and-prescriptive-agenda-for-research-on-mind-w2761197918--79c0d54db997'\n\nclaims = [\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-mindfulness-hype-exceeds-evidence--96cb023af909',\n        'text': 'Mindfulness and meditation have become widely promoted in clinical, educational, corporate, military, and media contexts despite an evidence base that remains uneven and vulnerable to exaggeration.',\n        'short_title': 'Hype exceeds evidence',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Fig. 1 tracks scientific and news media articles on mindfulness and/or meditation by year from 1970 to 2015, using Scopus for empirical scientific articles and LexisNexis for newspaper media pieces.',\n            'During the past two decades, mindfulness meditation has gone from being a fringe topic of scientific investigation to being an occasional replacement for psychotherapy, tool of corporate well-being, widely implemented educational practice, and “key to building more resilient soldiers.”'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-mindfulness-definition-is-ambiguous--4f220365d6f4',\n        'text': 'The term mindfulness lacks a universally accepted technical definition and often conflates heterogeneous mental faculties, practices, interventions, and measurement targets.',\n        'short_title': 'Mindfulness is ambiguous',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'There is neither one universally accepted technical definition of “mindfulness” nor any broad agreement about detailed aspects of the underlying concept to which it refers.',\n            'A 5-minute meditation exercise from a popular phone application might be treated the same as a 3-month meditation retreat, and a self-report questionnaire might be equated with the characteristics of someone who has spent decades practicing.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-self-report-measures-lack-validity--56550542660a',\n        'text': 'Self-report mindfulness questionnaires often have weak construct validity because scale meanings, factor structures, item interpretation, social desirability, and introspective limitations vary across participants and practice histories.',\n        'short_title': 'Self reports lack validity',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Table 1 lists multiple mindfulness measures with different theoretical contexts and factor structures, including FMI, MAAS, KIMS, FFMQ, TMS, CAMS-R, PHLMS, SMQ, and SMS.',\n            'Several of these scales exhibit different factor structures and response properties between meditators and nonmeditators, as well as before and after mindfulness training.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-clinical-evidence-remains-preliminary--117717456958',\n        'text': 'Clinical evidence for mindfulness-based interventions remains preliminary because much intervention research has not advanced beyond early-stage efficacy designs and often lacks active controls or real-world practice tests.',\n        'short_title': 'Clinical evidence remains preliminary',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Dimidjian and Segal estimate that only 30% of research using mindfulness-based interventions has moved beyond Stage 1; 20% was Stage 2a, 9% Stage 2b, and only 1% outside research contexts.',\n            'MBIs compared to active controls were found to have a mixture of only moderate, low, or no efficacy, depending on the disorder being treated.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-therapeutic-benefits-are-condition-specific--201401200700',\n        'text': 'Mindfulness-based therapies may help some people and conditions, particularly some depression-related contexts, but they should not be treated as broadly superior to established treatments.',\n        'short_title': 'Benefits are condition specific',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'MBCT may be modestly helpful for some individuals with residual symptoms of depression, and some guidelines endorse MBCT for preventing relapse in remitted patients with three or more previous depressive episodes.',\n            'Head-to-head comparisons of MBIs to other evidence-based practices have resulted in mixed findings, and direct comparisons of MBIs to empirically established treatments are limited.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-adverse-effects-are-undermeasured--25fa20260426',\n        'text': 'Meditation-related adverse effects and opportunity costs are plausible, documented, and undermeasured, so safety claims based on passive reporting or absent evidence are unwarranted.',\n        'short_title': 'Adverse effects are undermeasured',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Meditation-related experiences serious or distressing enough to warrant additional treatment or medical attention have been reported in more than 20 published case reports or observational studies.',\n            'Compared to 100% of pharmacology trials, less than 25% of meditation trials actively assess adverse events, relying instead on spontaneous reporting.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-neuroimaging-requires-caution--201770958900',\n        'text': 'Neuroimaging evidence on meditation can suggest modest brain differences, but group averages, cross-sectional designs, cardiorespiratory artifacts, head motion, and interpretive oversimplification limit causal and practical conclusions.',\n        'short_title': 'Neuroimaging requires caution',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Representative pictures from fMRI and other neuroimaging methods do not clearly convey the complex chain of biological and computational steps that lead to inferences about brain structure and function.',\n            'Many neuroimaging studies have used cross-sectional designs, precluding possible inferences about underlying cause-and-effect relationships.'\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        'claim_id': 'claim-research-needs-prescriptive-reform--79c0d54db997',\n        'text': 'Future mindfulness research should use precise terminology, stronger construct measures, active controls, preregistration, adverse-event monitoring, detailed intervention reporting, and cautious public communication.',\n        'short_title': 'Research needs reform',\n        'evidence_excerpts': [\n            'Table 3 proposes study design features for mindfulness-based interventions, including teacher information, practice information, instructor adherence, control group use, randomization, adverse-event monitoring, participant criteria, prior meditation experience, and conflicts of interest.',\n            'Researchers should adopt more nuanced terminology, follow replication-crisis lessons such as preregistered experiments and open-science replications, and report contemplative-neuroscience findings with due modesty.'\n        ]\n    }\n]\n\nevidence_items = [\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-publication-and-media-growth--96cb023af909',\n        'title': 'Publication and Media Growth',\n        'excerpt': 'Fig. 1. Scientific and news media articles on mindfulness and/or meditation by year from 1970 to 2015. Empirical scientific articles with the term mindfulness or meditation in the abstract, title, or keywords were searched using Scopus; media pieces with the term mindfulness or meditation in newspapers were searched using LexisNexis with a similarity filter.',\n        'overview': 'This figure is the paper’s central descriptive evidence for the claim that mindfulness moved rapidly from a fringe research topic into a saturated scientific and media discourse. It does not by itself prove that public claims are false, but it establishes the scale and timing of attention that makes hype, overgeneralization, and communication failures scientifically important.',\n        'evidence_type': 'figure_result',\n        'claim_refs': ['0'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['Fig. 1', 'p. 37']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-mindfulness-measures-table--4f220365d6f4',\n        'title': 'Divergent Mindfulness Measures',\n        'excerpt': 'Table 1 lists mindfulness measures with different contexts and factors: FMI from Buddhist theory with one general factor; MAAS from self-determination theory with attentiveness and awareness; KIMS from dialectical behavior therapy with observing, describing, awareness, and acceptance; FFMQ with nonreactivity, observing, awareness, describing, and nonjudging; TMS with curiosity and decentering; CAMS-R with attention, present focus, awareness, and acceptance.',\n        'overview': 'The table functions as concrete evidence that mindfulness is not measured as a single settled construct. Multiple instruments use different theoretical backgrounds and factor structures, making it risky to treat questionnaire scores as interchangeable evidence for a common mental capacity or intervention mechanism.',\n        'evidence_type': 'table_result',\n        'claim_refs': ['1', '2'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['Table 1', 'p. 39']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-clinical-stage-distribution--117717456958',\n        'title': 'Early Clinical Stage Distribution',\n        'excerpt': 'Dimidjian and Segal estimate, using the NIH stage model for clinical science, that only 30% of research using mindfulness-based interventions has moved beyond Stage 1. The majority beyond Stage 1 was Stage 2a, compared to wait-list control or treatment as usual; 9% of the total was Stage 2b, compared to active control; only 1% was conducted outside research contexts.',\n        'overview': 'These proportions are concrete methodological evidence for the paper’s caution about clinical rollout. They show that the intervention literature was dominated by early refinement or weak comparison designs, with very little evidence from active-control or real-world implementation contexts.',\n        'evidence_type': 'statistical_result',\n        'claim_refs': ['3'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['p. 45']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-active-control-efficacy-profile--117717456959',\n        'title': 'Mixed Active-Control Efficacy',\n        'excerpt': 'In a review and meta-analysis commissioned by the U.S. Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality, mindfulness-based interventions compared to active controls had only moderate efficacy for anxiety, depression, and pain; low efficacy for stress and quality of life; and no effect or insufficient evidence for attention, positive mood, substance abuse, eating habits, sleep, and weight control.',\n        'overview': 'This evidence item qualifies popular therapeutic claims by separating domains with moderate evidence from domains with weak or absent evidence. It supports a condition-specific interpretation: mindfulness interventions may be useful in some contexts, but the source rejects broad claims of strong or universal clinical efficacy.',\n        'evidence_type': 'statistical_result',\n        'claim_refs': ['3', '4'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['p. 46']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-adverse-event-reporting-gap--25fa20260426',\n        'title': 'Adverse Event Reporting Gap',\n        'excerpt': 'Meditation-related experiences serious or distressing enough to warrant additional treatment or medical attention have been reported in more than 20 published case reports or observational studies, including psychosis, mania, depersonalization, anxiety, panic, traumatic-memory reexperiencing, and clinical deterioration. Less than 25% of meditation trials actively assess adverse events, compared to 100% of pharmacology trials.',\n        'overview': 'The case-report count and trial-monitoring comparison are the paper’s strongest safety evidence. They do not establish population incidence, but they directly undermine claims that meditation is risk-free and show why passive reporting is inadequate for estimating harms.',\n        'evidence_type': 'statistical_result',\n        'claim_refs': ['5'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['pp. 47-48']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-study-design-checklist--79c0d54db997',\n        'title': 'Mindfulness Study Design Checklist',\n        'excerpt': 'Table 3 lists study design features for a mindfulness-based intervention: teacher information, practice information, physical and social settings, overall duration, meeting frequency and length, types and percentages of formal practice, informal practice, logs, instructional materials, instructor adherence, control group use, randomization/allocation method, adverse events monitored, inclusion/exclusion criteria, prior meditation experience, and conflicts of interest.',\n        'overview': 'The checklist is a prescriptive artifact rather than an outcome result. It concretely specifies the reporting and design details needed to make mindfulness studies interpretable, comparable, and safer, especially when different interventions share the same umbrella label.',\n        'evidence_type': 'formal_argument',\n        'claim_refs': ['1', '3', '7'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['Table 3', 'p. 42']\n    },\n    {\n        'evidence_id': 'evidence-neuroimaging-confounds--201770958900',\n        'title': 'Neuroimaging Confound Limits',\n        'excerpt': 'The source identifies neuroimaging limits including group-average inference, pooling diverse practitioners, cross-sectional designs that preclude cause-and-effect conclusions, head movement, cardiorespiratory effects, vein-drain artifacts, and difficulty estimating practical significance of observed structural or functional brain changes.',\n        'overview': 'This evidence item grounds the paper’s warning that meditation brain images are not self-interpreting proof of clinical benefit or causal neural change. The source acknowledges modest meta-analytic findings while arguing that artifacts and design limits must be handled before practical conclusions are imported into treatment or public messaging.',\n        'evidence_type': 'formal_argument',\n        'claim_refs': ['6', '7'],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'page_refs': ['pp. 50-51']\n    }\n]\n\ndata = {\n    'source_date_inference': {\n        'confidence': 'high',\n        'date': '2018',\n        'origin': 'source_markdown_publication_date',\n        'rationale': 'The source markdown cover page and citation state Publication date: 2018 for the Perspectives on Psychological Science article.'\n    },\n    'source': {\n        'display_title': 'Mindfulness Research Needs Less Hype and More Rigor',\n        'authors': [\n            'Nicholas T. Van Dam',\n            'Marieke K. van Vugt',\n            'David R. Vago',\n            'Laura Schmalzl',\n            'Clifford D. Saron',\n            'Andrew Olendzki',\n            'Ted Meissner',\n            'Sara W. Lazar',\n            'Catherine E. Kerr',\n            'Jolie Gorchov',\n            'Kieran C. R. Fox',\n            'Brent A. Field',\n            'Willoughby B. Britton',\n            'Julie A. Brefczynski-Lewis',\n            'David E. Meyer'\n        ],\n        'source_id': source_id,\n        'canonical_title': 'Mind the Hype: A Critical Evaluation and Prescriptive Agenda for Research on Mindfulness and Meditation',\n        'doi': '10.1177/1745691617709589',\n        'publication': 'Perspectives on Psychological Science'\n    },\n    'claims': claims,\n    'evidence_items': evidence_items,\n    'relations': [\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'supports',\n            'src_claim_ref': '1',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '2',\n            'rationale': 'Ambiguous definitions make self-report measures harder to interpret as measures of a single construct.'\n        },\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'supports',\n            'src_claim_ref': '3',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '4',\n            'rationale': 'The preliminary clinical evidence base constrains claims about broad therapeutic benefit.'\n        },\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'supports',\n            'src_claim_ref': '5',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '0',\n            'rationale': 'Undermeasured adverse effects raise the stakes of public hype and overconfident implementation.'\n        },\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'derived_from',\n            'src_claim_ref': '7',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '1',\n            'rationale': 'The recommendation for precise terminology follows from the paper’s diagnosis of semantic ambiguity.'\n        },\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'derived_from',\n            'src_claim_ref': '7',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '3',\n            'rationale': 'The recommendation for stronger study design follows from the weaknesses in clinical intervention methodology.'\n        },\n        {\n            'relation_type': 'derived_from',\n            'src_claim_ref': '7',\n            'dst_claim_ref': '6',\n            'rationale': 'The recommendation for cautious public communication follows from the limits of neuroimaging interpretation.'\n        }\n    ],\n    'summary': 'Van Dam and colleagues argue that mindfulness research has been promoted faster than its evidence base can support. The source identifies semantic ambiguity, weak measurement, immature clinical trials, underreported adverse effects, and overinterpreted neuroimaging as major reasons for a more cautious and rigorous research agenda.',\n    'warnings': [\n        {\n            'code': 'task_context_date_unresolved',\n            'message': 'The task_context reported date=null, but the source markdown itself states a 2018 publication date; the output uses the markdown evidence.'\n        },\n        {\n            'code': 'markdown_layout_artifacts',\n            'message': 'The pdftotext markdown is usable but includes two-column layout interleaving in some passages; extracted claims rely on repeated, clear passages and table captions rather than ambiguous line joins.'\n        }\n    ],\n    'source_dossier': {\n        'summary_short': 'This article is a critical review and agenda-setting paper about mindfulness and meditation research. It argues that enthusiasm in media, clinical practice, education, and neuroscience has outrun the precision and strength of the evidence. The authors call for clearer terminology, stronger measures, better clinical designs, active monitoring of harms, and more cautious communication of brain-imaging findings.',\n        'summary_long': 'Mind the Hype evaluates the modern mindfulness movement from the standpoint of psychological science, clinical intervention research, adverse-event monitoring, and contemplative neuroscience. Its central argument is not that mindfulness is useless or harmful in general; rather, it argues that public and institutional claims about mindfulness often exceed what the evidence can responsibly support. The article first identifies a semantic problem: “mindfulness” can refer to mental traits, temporary states, formal meditation practices, intervention packages, self-report scales, or long-term contemplative training. Because those uses differ so much, findings about one form of practice or measurement cannot automatically generalize to another. The authors then show how this ambiguity affects measurement. Existing questionnaires come from different theoretical traditions, use different factor structures, and may be interpreted differently by meditators and nonmeditators. Clinical evidence receives a similarly cautious treatment. The review recognizes possible benefits for some depression-related and symptom domains, but it emphasizes that much intervention work remains early-stage, often uses wait-list or treatment-as-usual controls, and only rarely tests real-world implementation. Safety is another major theme: meditation-related adverse experiences have been documented, yet trials have often relied on passive reporting, making “no evidence of harm” a weak basis for public reassurance. Finally, the article warns that neuroimaging evidence is especially vulnerable to oversimplification because brain images depend on complex group-level, statistical, physiological, and design assumptions. The prescriptive agenda is therefore multipronged: define practices and constructs precisely, report intervention details, use active controls and preregistration, include skeptical and multidisciplinary teams, systematically assess adverse events, and communicate findings to the public without exaggerating benefits or certainty.',\n        'sections': [\n            {\n                'heading': 'Central Argument',\n                'body': 'The source argues that mindfulness research has generated genuine scientific interest, but that public enthusiasm, media messaging, and some applied rollouts have moved faster than the evidence. Its critical stance is balanced: mindfulness may help some people in some contexts, yet the authors reject claims that it is a broadly proven remedy or a risk-free replacement for established care.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-mindfulness-hype-exceeds-evidence--96cb023af909', 'claim-therapeutic-benefits-are-condition-specific--201401200700']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Definition Problem',\n                'body': 'A major source of confusion is the umbrella use of “mindfulness.” The paper treats semantic precision as a methodological requirement, because a brief phone-app exercise, an eight-week MBSR course, a self-report questionnaire, and decades of contemplative practice are not interchangeable evidence targets. Without differentiating practices, mechanisms, and measures, studies are difficult to compare.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-mindfulness-definition-is-ambiguous--4f220365d6f4']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Measurement Path',\n                'body': 'The discussion of questionnaires shows why simple mindfulness scores can mislead. The listed measures differ in origins and factor structures, and the authors highlight problems with introspection, social desirability, demand characteristics, and different item meanings before versus after training. The recommended path is to measure more specific cognitive, affective, behavioral, and biological capacities rather than treating mindfulness as a unitary latent trait.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-self-report-measures-lack-validity--56550542660a', 'claim-research-needs-prescriptive-reform--79c0d54db997']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Clinical Evidence',\n                'body': 'The article distinguishes limited, condition-specific promise from generalized clinical efficacy. It notes possible benefits in some depression-related contexts and some symptom outcomes, but the broader evidence base is constrained by early-stage designs, limited active controls, inconsistent intervention content, and very little research outside controlled research settings. The result is a call for caution before mainstream implementation.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-clinical-evidence-remains-preliminary--117717456958', 'claim-therapeutic-benefits-are-condition-specific--201401200700']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Safety and Harms',\n                'body': 'The source treats adverse effects as an underdeveloped research area rather than a settled objection to meditation. Case reports and observational studies document serious distressing experiences, while trial monitoring has often been weak. The authors also emphasize opportunity cost: overstated claims may divert vulnerable people from better established treatment or ordinary activities with known mental and physical benefits.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-adverse-effects-are-undermeasured--25fa20260426']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Neuroscience Limits',\n                'body': 'Contemplative neuroscience receives a nuanced assessment. Brain imaging can generate promising hypotheses and meta-analytic patterns, but the paper warns against translating colorful images into simple claims about benefit, causality, or clinical importance. Group averaging, cross-sectional comparisons, respiration, cardiac activity, head movement, and lifestyle differences can all affect interpretation.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-neuroimaging-requires-caution--201770958900']\n            },\n            {\n                'heading': 'Practical Interpretation',\n                'body': 'The practical lesson is reform rather than rejection. The authors want mindfulness research to adopt clearer taxonomies, stronger reporting checklists, active control groups, preregistration, multidisciplinary and skeptical collaboration, adverse-event assessment, and careful public communication. Readers should treat mindfulness as a family of practices requiring context-specific evidence, not as a single validated intervention with universal effects.',\n                'grounding_claim_ids': ['claim-research-needs-prescriptive-reform--79c0d54db997', 'claim-mindfulness-hype-exceeds-evidence--96cb023af909']\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n}\n\noutput.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\noutput.write_text(json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2) + '\\\\n', encoding='utf-8')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit